INTRODUCTION
============
The major differences to most BSD derived FTP daemons are:
- stand-alone, not called by inetd
- small memory footprint
- multi-threaded, pre-forking
- supports traffic shaping
- highly configurable using access control lists for commands and
  configuration variables

Some wu-ftpd-like features (banners, checksum calculation) are available.
No DNS resolving is currently done.

Most of the commands from RFC959 are supported. Additionally, the
following extensions and/or commands are implemented:
- FOOBAR (RFC1639), RFC2428
- SSL (both via AUTH SSL and port 990)
- MDTM, SIZE, LANG, HOST, MLST, MLSD, FEAT, OPTS
- various SITE commands

IPv6 support isn't well tested yet, but may work.

Under certain conditions (e.g. locally copying data from a RAM disk to
/dev/null), this daemon is somewhat slower than the BSD-derived ones.
That's because the BSD daemons just call read(2) and write(2) until a
file is transfered, but because of its multi-threaded nature, this daemon
needs to call poll(2) before doing I/O operations.

